MAJOR AIMS for the v3.Y versions (from v3.2): large systems, up to 1 billion atoms
Aim: UMD runs on systems of up to 10^9 atoms (large classical and machine-learning
potential runs), on a workstation for 10^7-10^8 atoms and on a cluster node beyond.

Scale. An atom line of a UMD file has ~130 bytes: one snapshot of 10^9 atoms is ~130 GB
of text, more than the memory of a workstation (128 GB); reading it in C takes ~3 min per
snapshot on one core, and 1000 snapshots are 130 TB. Text is not the format at that size.

What blocks it now (November 2026):
- c_gofr compares all the pairs of atoms (N^2): ~10^10 pairs per snapshot at 10^5 atoms
- c_clusters finds the polymerized clusters (-r 0) by recursion, one call deeper per atom:
  a network across the cell of 10^5-10^6 atoms overflows the stack; is_in scans the
  element ranges for every bond
- 32-bit integers for the bond counts and the text lengths (c_UMDprocess defineBonds,
  c_clusters, c_bonds_full): overflow at ~5x10^8 atoms (4x10^9 bonds) or 2 GB of bonding
  text per snapshot
- speciation_and_angles.py keeps the bonds of every snapshot in memory (atoms x snapshots)
- msd_umd.py and vibrations_umd.py read the whole trajectory at once (read_values:
  atoms x snapshots x 24 bytes)
- text formats with one line per atom (.umd.dat, .bonding.dat): unworkable beyond ~10^8 atoms

Plan, one v3.Y release per step, each checked against the validation suite:
v3.2 - measure and remove the hard limits (no change of format):
  - large synthetic runs in validation/synthetic.py (fcc, ideal gas; 10^5, 10^6, 10^7
    atoms) and a --large level of the validation suite
  - time and peak memory of every script against the number of atoms: where each breaks
  - 64-bit counts, indices and lengths in the C libraries
  - clusters by union-find (no recursion, nearly linear in the number of bonds)
v3.3 - linear time per snapshot:
  - g(r) with sub-cells (neighbour lists), as c_bonds_full for the bonds; the histograms
    are integer counts, so the results stay identical
  - g(r), bonding and clusters all linear in the number of atoms
v3.4 - memory bounded by a budget, not by the length of the run:
  - speciation keeps only the species alive and writes each one when its life ends
  - MSD, vibrations and cluster MSDs by chunks of atoms, one pass over the file per chunk
    (read_atoms_at with a range of atoms)
  - optional MSD on a random sample of the atoms (enough for D)
v3.5 - a binary format, as a new file type (e.g. <base>.umdb), never a change of .umd.dat:
  a .umd.dat without its atom lines would break the readers of former versions
  - text header and snapshot fields; the atoms in fixed-width binary columns; an index of
    the snapshot offsets; read by memory mapping (numpy memmap)
  - conversion in both directions for small runs; LAMMPSParser2umd.py writes it directly
  - an atom-major copy for MSD and vibrations (the whole time series of an atom at once)
  - a binary bonding file (lists of bonds per snapshot)
  - binary is not much smaller than text (96 bytes per atom in double precision against
    ~130): the gains are no parsing, only the columns needed, and fast access by atom
v3.6 - one snapshot split by regions (domain decomposition): workers take slabs of the cell
  with a margin of r_max or of the bond cutoff; clusters crossing the slab borders joined by
  union-find; needs the binary format, to read a slab without parsing the whole snapshot

Language: C with worker processes through the v3.Y versions (no new dependency, the users
already compile the C libraries); numba or CuPy against C decided with the GPU work (v4.0).

Tests: up to ~10^8 atoms for a few snapshots on a workstation (~13 GB of text per snapshot);
10^9 atoms need a cluster node and a real large run (LAMMPS, 10^7 atoms or more).

Open decisions:
- binary format: raw numpy arrays with a text header (no new dependency) or HDF5 (h5py,
  readable by other tools)
- outputs at 10^9 atoms: every cluster in the popul file and every bond in the bonding file,
  or, above a size, statistics only (counts, distributions of lifetimes)
- the dump format of the large runs to parse

